 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Spectral Bias:** Without FFM, the eigenvalues of the NTK for standard MLPs with ReLU activations decrease rapidly, causing high-frequency components to be learned very slowly, resulting in no sharp edges [S3, unofficial][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Bandwidth Selection:** The choice of $\sigma$ (or $\alpha$) is critical. A small $\alpha$ (high frequency) causes high output variance, resulting in noisy artifacts and spiky (under-smoothed) estimates. A large $\alpha$ introduces high bias, leading to over-smoothed predictions and loss of high-frequency details [S4].
+*   **Spectral Bias:** Without FFM, the eigenvalues of the NTK for standard MLPs with ReLU activations decrease rapidly, causing high-frequency components to be learned very slowly, resulting in no sharp edges [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Bandwidth Selection:** The choice of $\sigma$ (or $\alpha$) is critical [S4]. A small $\alpha$ (high frequency) causes high output variance, resulting in noisy artifacts and spiky (under-smoothed) estimates [S4]. A large $\alpha$ introduces high bias, leading to over-smoothed predictions and loss of high-frequency details [S4].
 *   **Grid Search:** Finding the optimal bandwidth often requires a time-consuming grid-search scheme, leading to inefficient training overload [S4].
-*   **Isotropy Limitation:** Standard FFM uses a single global bandwidth, which treats all input dimensions equally. This fails to handle signals with heterogeneous frequency characteristics across dimensions (e.g., high spatial variation but low angular variation), leading to imbalanced regression [S4].
